(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-anatomy-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do \"ipsilateral\" and \"contralateral\" denote?","choices":["Ipsilateral denotes the same side; contralateral denotes the opposite side","Both terms describe superior/inferior relationships","Ipsilateral denotes the opposite side; contralateral denotes the same side","Both terms describe anterior/posterior relationships"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ipsilateral denotes (of) the same side, while contralateral denotes (on) the opposite side. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"According to the material, most anatomical descriptions of position are expressed relative to what reference?","choices":["The position used only in radiographic imaging","The anatomical position","An arbitrary reference chosen per description","The position the person happens to be in at the time"],"answer":1,"exp":"All anatomical descriptions are expressed in relation to the anatomical position, regardless of the actual position a person may be in. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the anatomical position, how are the lower limbs, feet, and toes oriented?","choices":["Positioned with the feet spread apart and turned outward laterally","Positioned close together, with the toes directed toward the back of the body","Close together with the feet parallel and toes directed anteriorly","Crossed at the ankles with toes turned medially"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the anatomical position, the lower limbs are close together with the feet parallel and the toes directed anteriorly. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the anatomical position, where are the head, eyes, and toes directed?","choices":["Anterior (forward)","Superior (upward)","Laterally (outward)","Posterior (backward)"],"answer":0,"exp":"In the anatomical position, the head, eyes, and toes are directed anterior (forward). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which term describes the intermediate position \"nearer to the head and farther from the median plane,\" with the superolateral surface of the brain as the given example?","choices":["Superomedial","Inferolateral","Superolateral","Inferomedial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Superolateral means nearer to the head and farther from the median plane; the example given is the superolateral surface of the brain. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does clinical anatomy emphasize?","choices":["Only the technique of cadaveric dissection, without regard to living patients","Structure and function as it relates to the practice of medicine","Only embryological development of the tissues","Only the classification scheme used for the organ systems of the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Clinical anatomy emphasizes structure and function as it relates to the practice of medicine. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which pair of terms describes an area's distance from a point of reference, most often the body's center in general use?","choices":["Rostral and caudal","Superficial and deep","Ipsilateral and contralateral","Proximal and distal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proximal (closest to a point of reference) and distal (farthest from a point of reference) describe distance from a reference point, generally the body's center. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which term pair is often used interchangeably with anterior/posterior, respectively, particularly in neurological contexts?","choices":["Palmar and plantar","Proximal and distal","Rostral and caudal","Superficial and deep"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rostral is sometimes used interchangeably with anterior, and caudal is sometimes used interchangeably with posterior, particularly in neurological terms. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as an example of a regional anatomy division?","choices":["The nervous system","The endocrine system","Thorax and abdomen","The circulatory system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Thorax and abdomen is one of the regional divisions listed (alongside head and neck, back, pelvis, and upper and lower extremities). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which term describes lying on the ventral surface, face down?","choices":["Supine","Caudal","Prone","Rostral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Prone means lying on the ventral surface (face down). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is elastic connective tissue composed of, and what are examples of where it's found?","choices":["Composed predominantly of collagen fibers; typically found within tendons","Composed of mostly elastin fibers; found in vocal cords and ligamenta flava","Composed of ground substance only; found in the membrane","Composed of mineral crystals such as calcium salts; typically found within bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elastic connective tissue is composed of mostly elastin fibers, found in structures like the vocal cords and ligamenta flava (vertebral connective tissue). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"According to the material, what is the most abundant and widely distributed tissue type?","choices":["Epithelial tissue","Muscle tissue","Nervous tissue","Connective tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Connective tissue is described as the most abundant and widely distributed tissue. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What ground substance component is described as a \"slippery polysaccharide\" that reduces friction?","choices":["Calcium hydroxyapatite","Elastin","Collagen","Hyaluronic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hyaluronic acid is described as a slippery polysaccharide, part of the ground substance component of the connective tissue matrix. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What do glycosaminoglycans do within the ground substance, per the material?","choices":["Produce the myelin sheaths that wrap around peripheral nerve fibers","Carry a positive charge and actively repel surrounding water molecules","Negatively charged, bind Na+ and K+, and attract water","Form the rigid mineral crystal component embedded within bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glycosaminoglycans are negatively charged, bind Na+ and K+, and attract water (H2O) within the ground substance. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are proteoglycan aggregates described as, structurally, within the ground substance?","choices":["Pine tree-shaped molecules","Flat, scale-shaped structures","Coiled, spring-shaped structures","Branching, network-forming structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Proteoglycan aggregates are described as pine tree-shaped molecules, part of the ground substance. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What cell type contains heparin and histamine, per the material's list of connective tissue cells?","choices":["Mast cells","Macrophages","Fibroblasts","Adipose cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mast cells are described as containing heparin and histamine. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is notable about the regeneration potential and one surface exposure of epithelial cells?","choices":["Exhibits a high capacity for regeneration, with both surfaces embedded within surrounding connective tissue","High regeneration potential, with one side always exposed to the body exterior or an organ cavity","Exhibits a low capacity for regeneration, with one side that is always richly vascularized","Exhibits a low capacity for regeneration, with no surface ever exposed to a cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Epithelial cells have high regeneration potential, and one side is always exposed to either the body exterior or an organ cavity. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of the following describes a listed location of epithelial tissue?","choices":["Covers the body and lines the cavities, tubes, ducts, and blood vessels inside the body","Found exclusively within the muscular walls that line hollow internal organs","Found only within the ventricles of the central nervous system","Found only within the red and yellow marrow cavities of bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epithelial tissue covers the body, lines the cavities, tubes, ducts, and blood vessels inside the body, and covers the organs inside body cavities. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does the \"fluid\" component of the connective tissue matrix contain?","choices":["Gases and nutrients, plus minerals such as calcium salts","Only myelin produced by supporting glial cells of the nervous system","Consists solely of the living blast, cyte, and clast cell types","Consists solely of collagen and elastin protein fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fluid component of the connective tissue matrix contains gases and nutrients, along with minerals such as calcium salts. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Besides mast cells and adipose cells, which other cell types are listed among the living components of connective tissue?","choices":["Squamous and cuboidal cells","Macrophages and white blood cells","Neurons and their glial cells","Only chondrocytes and osteocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Macrophages and white blood cells are listed among the living cellular components of connective tissue, alongside blast/cyte/clast cells, mast cells, and adipose cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the corpora quadrigemina, and what are its two pairs of nuclei responsible for?","choices":["Part of the cerebellum entirely rather than the midbrain, responsible solely for balance and coordinated fine motor movement patterns overall","Two pairs of nuclei located in the medulla instead of the midbrain; both process only motor output signals exclusively, per the material","A single nucleus located within the pons instead of the midbrain, responsible solely for regulating breathing and respiratory rate","Two pairs of nuclei in the midbrain; the inferior colliculus processes auditory stimuli and the superior colliculus processes visual stimuli"],"answer":3,"exp":"The corpora quadrigemina consists of two pairs of nuclei in the midbrain, with auditory processing occurring in the inferior colliculus and visual processing occurring in the superior colliculus. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What do the red nucleus and substantia nigra do in the midbrain?","choices":["Red nucleus relays visual reflex signals from the superior colliculus; substantia nigra relays auditory reflex signals from the inferior colliculus","Red nucleus integrates information between the cerebrum and cerebellum; substantia nigra regulates motor output of the basal nuclei","Red nucleus produces cerebrospinal fluid via its own choroid plexus tissue; substantia nigra reabsorbs it through arachnoid granulations","Red nucleus and substantia nigra together form the apneustic and pneumotaxic centers that regulate involuntary breathing rhythm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The red nucleus integrates information between the cerebrum and cerebellum, while the substantia nigra regulates motor output of the basal nuclei. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the function of the cerebellar cortex, and what does the arbor vitae do?","choices":["Cerebellar cortex provides subconscious coordination of movements; arbor vitae connects the cerebellar cortex with the cerebellar peduncles","Cerebellar cortex regulates the rhythmic breathing centers; arbor vitae produces cerebrospinal fluid within the fourth ventricle","Cerebellar cortex controls emotional responses; arbor vitae forms part of the limbic system's connecting fiber tracts","Cerebellar cortex processes visual stimuli; arbor vitae relays signals from the thalamus into the cerebrum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cerebellar cortex provides subconscious coordination of movements, and the arbor vitae (the branching white matter pattern) connects the cerebellar cortex with the cerebellar peduncles. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What are the three cerebellar peduncles, and what does each connect?","choices":["Superior, middle, and inferior peduncles, with all three connecting the cerebellum only to the cerebral cortex above","Superior (connects cerebellum with mesencephalon/diencephalon/cerebrum), middle (communicates with the pons), and inferior (connects with the medulla oblongata)","Anterior, posterior, and lateral peduncles, all three connecting the cerebellum directly to the spinal cord","Only a single fused peduncle exists in this scheme, connecting the cerebellum to both the spinal cord and the medulla directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three cerebellar peduncles are the superior (connects the cerebellum with the mesencephalon, diencephalon, and cerebrum), middle (communicates between the cerebellum and pons), and inferior (connection between the cerebellum and medulla oblongata). — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What nuclei and centers does the pons contain?","choices":["Sensory and motor nuclei for CN I and II only, plus centers controlling voluntary eye movement","Only nuclei associated with taste sensation and the regulation of salivary gland secretion are present","Sensory and motor nuclei for CN V, VI, VII, and VIII, plus apneustic and pneumotaxic centers for involuntary breathing control","Only nuclei relaying visual information received from the superior colliculus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pons contains sensory and motor nuclei for CN V, VI, VII, and VIII, nuclei involved with involuntary control of breathing (apneustic and pneumotaxic centers), and nuclei that relay cerebellar commands. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where is the medulla oblongata located, and what passes through it?","choices":["The most caudal segment of the brainstem, lying just above the foramen magnum; all communication from the brain and spinal cord passes through here","Located within the cerebellar vermis, connecting the two cerebellar hemispheres to each other","Located within the diencephalon, situated immediately superior to the thalamus and posterior to the hypothalamus","The most rostral segment of the entire brainstem, lying directly below the corpus callosum and fornix"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medulla oblongata is the most caudal segment of the brainstem, lying just above the foramen magnum, and all communication from the brain and spinal cord passes through here. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What autonomic centers are located in the medulla oblongata's autonomic nuclei?","choices":["Centers for processing visual and auditory stimuli","Cardiovascular and respiratory centers (cardiac, vasomotor, and rhythmic breathing)","Centers for voluntary muscle control and coordination","Centers for long-term memory storage and retrieval"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medulla oblongata's autonomic nuclei include cardiovascular and respiratory centers — cardiac, vasomotor, and rhythmic breathing centers. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What are the three layers of the cranial meninges, from outer to inner?","choices":["Only two layers exist: dura mater and pia mater","Dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater","Arachnoid mater, dura mater, and pia mater","Pia mater, arachnoid mater, and dura mater"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three layers of the cranial meninges, from outer to inner, are the dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the two layers of the dura mater, and what is the space between them called?","choices":["Superficial and deep layers; the space between them is called the subdural space","Endosteal/periosteal layer and meningeal layer; the space between them is the dural sinus","Arachnoid layer and pial layer; the space between them is called the subarachnoid space","Only one layer exists, the periosteal layer, with no space between anything"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dura mater consists of two layers — the endosteal/periosteal layer and the meningeal layer — with the space between them called the dural sinus. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the falx cerebri, and where does it attach?","choices":["A meningeal dural fold extending into the longitudinal fissure, attaching anteriorly to the crista galli and posteriorly to the internal occipital crest/tentorium cerebelli","A fold extending downward from the tentorium cerebelli, separating the two cerebellar hemispheres from one another","A fold separating the cerebellar hemispheres below from the overlying cerebral hemispheres above, running horizontally","A fold lining the sella turcica, forming a roof that encases and protects the pituitary gland resting below it"],"answer":0,"exp":"The falx cerebri is a meningeal layer fold that extends into the longitudinal fissure, with its inferior/anterior portion attaching to the crista galli and its inferior/posterior portion attaching to the internal occipital crest and tentorium cerebelli. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Why does it take many Schwann cells to myelinate an entire axon?","choices":["Schwann cells never myelinate axons, only oligodendrocytes do in the PNS","Schwann cells myelinate the entire axon length individually, requiring only one","Each Schwann cell myelinates 10 cm of axon","Each Schwann cell myelinates only about 1 mm of an axon"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because each Schwann cell myelinates only about 1 mm of an axon, it takes many Schwann cells to myelinate an entire axon. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Can a single Schwann cell myelinate multiple unmyelinated axons?","choices":["No — unmyelinated axons have no associated Schwann cells shielding them at any point along their length","Yes — a single Schwann cell shields multiple unmyelinated axons within superficial grooves, though a chain of Schwann cells is still needed to enclose an entire axon","No — a single Schwann cell can only ever be associated with one axon at a time, myelinated or not","Yes — a single Schwann cell is said to myelinate the entire length of several separate axons entirely alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"A single Schwann cell can shield multiple unmyelinated axons within superficial grooves, though a chain of Schwann cells is still needed to enclose an entire axon. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"During myelination of a peripheral axon, what happens as the Schwann cell rotates around the axon?","choices":["The inner membranous layers are compressed and the cytoplasm is forced into more superficial layers, leaving the myelin sheath as phospholipid bilayers with the Schwann cell nucleus/cytoplasm at the surface","The Schwann cell instead divides into two entirely separate daughter cells that wrap the axon, each forming its own distinct segment","The axon itself is said to rotate around a stationary Schwann cell, wrapping its own membrane around the cell repeatedly as it turns","The myelin sheath is said to form entirely from the axon's own plasma membrane, without any Schwann cell involvement whatsoever occurring"],"answer":0,"exp":"As the Schwann cell rotates around the axon, the inner membranous layers are compressed and the cytoplasm is forced into more superficial layers; when completed, the myelin sheath consists only of the phospholipid bilayers of the plasma membrane, with the Schwann cell nucleus and cytoplasm at the surface. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is a synapse, and what are the two major types?","choices":["The junction between a neuron and another cell; chemical (vesicular) and electrical (nonvesicular) synapses","A structure found only within the cell body, not at any junction between cells","Specifically the junction formed between two axons; classified as excitatory or inhibitory synapses","Specifically the junction formed between a neuron and bone tissue; classified as myelinated or unmyelinated synapses"],"answer":0,"exp":"A synapse is the junction between a neuron and another cell, and the two major types are the chemical synapse (vesicular) and the electrical synapse (nonvesicular). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In the CNS, what is a \"center,\" and what is it called if it has a distinct boundary?","choices":["A synapse; when it has a distinct boundary, it is instead referred to as a node","A bundle of axons; when it has a distinct boundary, it is instead referred to as a tract","A ganglion; when it has a distinct boundary, it is instead referred to as a column","A collection of cell bodies; if it has a distinct boundary, it is called a nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the CNS, a collection of cell bodies is called a center; if this center has a distinct boundary, it is called a nucleus. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the CNS, what do tracts and columns refer to?","choices":["Ganglia found in the PNS","Bundles of axons that make up white matter","Collections of cell bodies with a distinct boundary","Collections of cell bodies without a distinct boundary"],"answer":1,"exp":"White matter in the CNS consists of axons bundled together in tracts and columns. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the PNS, what are axons bundled together to form, and what are collections of cell bodies called?","choices":["Axons are bundled together to form columns; cell bodies cluster together to form centers","There is no organizational bundling structure in the PNS","Axons are bundled together to form tracts; cell bodies cluster together to form nuclei","Axons bundle into nerves; cell bodies are clustered in ganglia"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the PNS, axons are bundled together to form nerves, while collections of cell bodies are clustered in ganglia. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What are interneurons, and where are they located?","choices":["Located entirely in the PNS, connecting sensory receptors to effectors directly without CNS involvement","Located only within the ganglia, never appearing within either the CNS or PNS proper structures","Located entirely in the CNS, situated between motor and sensory neurons, analyzing sensory input and coordinating motor outputs","Functionally the same as afferent neurons, simply carrying a different descriptive name"],"answer":2,"exp":"Interneurons are located entirely in the CNS, situated between the motor and sensory neurons, analyzing sensory input and coordinating motor outputs; they can be excitatory or inhibitory. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the perikaryon, and what are axoplasm and collaterals?","choices":["Perikaryon, axoplasm, and collaterals all refer to the identical single structure","Perikaryon is the neuron cytoplasm (of the cell body); axoplasm is the cytoplasm of the axon; collaterals are branches off the axon","Perikaryon is instead classified as a type of neuroglia, not considered a true neuron structure at all","Perikaryon refers to the axon's cytoplasm; axoplasm refers to the cell body's cytoplasm; collaterals refer to the axon terminals"],"answer":1,"exp":"The perikaryon is the neuron cytoplasm, axoplasm is the cytoplasm of the axon specifically, and collaterals are branches off the axon. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are anaxonic neurons?","choices":["Neurons with more than two processes, where axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites","Neurons possessing exactly two processes, positioned on either side of the cell body","Neurons possessing a single elongated process, with the cell body positioned off to one side","Neurons found exclusively in the PNS, never the CNS at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Anaxonic neurons have more than two processes, but axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Where is the first-order neuron of the anterior (ventral) spinocerebellar tract found, and does it decussate?","choices":["Found from C8 down to S3; it ascends ipsilaterally without decussating at all, entering via the inferior cerebellar peduncle","Found in the cervical region only (C1-C7); decussates within the medulla oblongata before ascending","Found from C3 to T7; ascends via the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus in the medulla","Found from L1 to S2; decussates (contralateral) within the spinal cord in the anterior white commissure, entering the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The first-order neuron of the anterior spinocerebellar tract is found from L1 to S2, and it decussates (contralaterally) within the spinal cord in the anterior white commissure, entering the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the cuneocerebellar tract, and where is its first-order neuron found?","choices":["Transmits unconscious proprioceptive information for the upper extremity; first-order neuron found from C3 to T7, ascending within the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus","Transmits unconscious proprioceptive information for the lower extremity; the first-order neuron is found from C8 to S3, ascending ipsilaterally without decussation","A descending motor tract, not a sensory one, found only within the thoracic region from T1 to T7","Transmits conscious pain sensation for the lower extremity; first-order neuron found from L1 to S2, decussating centrally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cuneocerebellar tract transmits unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum for the entire upper extremity; its first-order neuron is found from C3 to T7, with axons ascending within the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus in the medulla, then a second-order neuron travels ipsilaterally to the inferior cerebellar peduncle. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Where is the intermediolateral gray horn located, and what does it contain?","choices":["Prominent only within the midsacral region (S2-S4); it contains the parasympathetic preganglionic cells for the pelvic organs","Lies between the dorsal and ventral gray columns, prominent in the thoracic and upper lumbar regions; contains preganglionic cells for the autonomic nervous system","Lies entirely within the anterior white commissure; contains only somatic lower motor neurons","Lies entirely within the dorsal columns of white matter; contains only sensory neuron cell bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"The intermediolateral gray column (horn) lies between the dorsal and ventral gray columns, forming a prominent lateral triangular projection in the thoracic and upper lumbar regions (but not the midsacral region), and contains preganglionic cells for the autonomic nervous system. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Within spinal segments T1 to L2/L3, what pathway do preganglionic sympathetic axons take after leaving the intermediolateral gray column?","choices":["Remain entirely within the spinal cord's white matter tracts, never reaching the periphery at all","Travel via the gray rami communicantes directly to the paravertebral ganglia, bypassing the ventral root","Leave the spinal cord within the ventral roots, then travel to the sympathetic ganglia via the white rami communicantes","Leave the spinal cord within the dorsal roots, traveling directly to target organs without any ganglia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons within the intermediolateral gray column give rise to sympathetic axons that leave the spinal cord within the ventral roots, then travel to the sympathetic ganglia via the white rami communicantes. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the difference between white rami communicantes and gray rami communicantes?","choices":["White rami carry postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve; gray rami carry preganglionic fibers and exist at all spinal levels","White and gray rami are simply two names for the exact same structure at every spinal level","White rami exist only at T1-L2/L3 and carry preganglionic fibers to the paravertebral ganglia; gray rami carry postganglionic fibers","Both white and gray rami carry identical postganglionic fiber types at every spinal level"],"answer":2,"exp":"White rami communicantes exist only at spinal levels where the intermediolateral cell column is present (T1-L2/L3) and carry preganglionic nerve fibers from the spinal cord to the paravertebral ganglia, while gray rami communicantes carry postganglionic nerve fibers. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the lateral corticospinal tract innervate, and where does it decussate?","choices":["Innervates axial muscles; decussates at the level of the spinal cord itself","Innervates the diaphragm only; decussates within the thalamus before descending","Innervates limbs for skilled movements; decussates in the medulla's pyramids","Innervates limbs for skilled movements; does not decussate at any level"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lateral corticospinal tract innervates limbs for skilled movements, with its signal originating in the primary motor cortex and decussating in the medulla's pyramids; in the spinal cord, the tract is located laterally. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the anterior corticospinal tract innervate, and where does it decussate?","choices":["Innervates limbs for skilled movements; decussates in the medulla's pyramids","Innervates the axial muscles; does not decussate","Innervates the limbs; decussates in the thalamus","Innervates the axial muscles; decussates at the spinal cord level"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior corticospinal tract innervates the axial muscles, with its signal originating in the primary motor cortex, and decussation occurring at the spinal cord level; in the spinal cord, the tract is located anteriorly along the anterior median fissure. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What sensory information does the anterior spinothalamic tract carry, and where does it decussate?","choices":["Pain, pressure, and temperature sensation; it decussates within the medulla, several levels above the point of cord entry","Crude touch sensation; decussates within the thalamus itself, well above cord entry","Crude touch sensation; decussates in the spinal cord itself, through the anterior commissure, usually 1-2 levels above cord entry","Fine touch and proprioception; does not decussate at any spinal cord level"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior spinothalamic tract carries crude touch sensation, decussating in the spinal cord itself through the anterior commissure, usually 1-2 levels above the level the sensory fiber enters the cord. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What sensory information does the lateral spinothalamic tract carry?","choices":["Only pain sensation, with pressure and temperature carried elsewhere","Pain, pressure, and temperature sensation","Crude touch sensation only","Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain, pressure, and temperature sensation, decussating in the spinal cord itself through the anterior commissure. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What pathway do the spinothalamic tracts follow from the posterior gray horn to the primary sensory cortex?","choices":["Enter the anterior gray horn directly, bypassing Lissauer's tract entirely, decussate within the thalamus itself, and then transmit back down to the medulla and sensory cortex","Enter the posterior gray horn through Lissauer's tract, decussate within the spinal cord, ascend to the medulla, transmit to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus, then to the primary sensory cortex","Enter the posterior gray horn through Lissauer's tract, then bypass the thalamus entirely, transmitting directly to the cortex","Enter the anterior gray horn, decussate in the medulla, and transmit directly to the cerebellum, bypassing the thalamus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sensory information enters the posterior gray horn through Lissauer's tract and decussates within the spinal cord itself, ascends to the medulla oblongata, transmits to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus, and is then transmitted to the primary sensory cortex. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the sciatic nerve innervate, per the material's listed muscles?","choices":["Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae","Gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus muscles","Semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and adductor magnus","Quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and iliopsoas muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sciatic nerve innervates the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and adductor magnus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the pudendal nerve innervate?","choices":["The diaphragm and phrenic-innervated abdominal structures","The quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and iliopsoas muscles","The perineum, external anal sphincter, and urethral sphincter","The gluteus maximus, piriformis, and obturator internus muscles only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pudendal nerve innervates the perineum, external anal sphincter, and urethral sphincter. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What sensory territory does the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve cover?","choices":["The anterior, lateral, and posterior thigh, excluding the perineum","The perineum, posterior thigh, and posterior leg","The neck, posterior scalp, and posterior head","The anteromedial thigh and external genitals only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve provides sensory information from the perineum, posterior thigh, and posterior leg. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is a reflex, and what is a reflex arc?","choices":["A reflex arc begins at a motor neuron and ends at a sensory receptor; a reflex is a consciously planned, voluntary motor response requiring cortical input","A reflex is the neural \"wiring\" itself; a reflex arc is the resulting involuntary motor response produced once the circuit is activated","A reflex is a voluntary, consciously controlled motor response; a reflex arc is the neural wiring of multiple reflexes combined into one circuit","A reflex is an immediate involuntary motor response; a reflex arc is the neural \"wiring\" of a single reflex, beginning at a sensory receptor and ending at a peripheral effector"],"answer":3,"exp":"A reflex is an immediate involuntary motor response, and a reflex arc is the neural \"wiring\" of a single reflex, beginning at a sensory receptor and ending at a peripheral effector. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the correct pathway of a reflex arc, in order?","choices":["Activation of a motor neuron → information processing → activation of a sensory receptor → response by the effector → relay to the CNS","Information processing → response by the effector → activation of a sensory receptor → relay to the CNS → activation of a motor neuron","Activation of a sensory receptor → relay of information to the CNS → information processing → activation of a motor neuron → response by the effector","Response by the effector → activation of a sensory receptor → relay to the CNS → information processing → activation of a motor neuron"],"answer":2,"exp":"The reflex arc pathway is: activation of a sensory receptor, relay of information to the CNS, information processing, activation of a motor neuron, and response by the effector. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Reflexes are classified according to which four criteria, per the material?","choices":["Only their development, classified strictly as either innate (genetically programmed) or acquired (learned through practice)","Development (innate/acquired), site of processing (spinal/cranial), nature of motor response (somatic/visceral/autonomic), and complexity (monosynaptic/polysynaptic)","Only the specific muscle group involved, categorized by upper limb, lower limb, or axial muscles","Only their complexity, classified strictly as either monosynaptic or polysynaptic circuits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Reflexes are classified according to their development (innate or acquired), the site where information is processed (spinal or cranial/cerebral), the nature of the resulting motor response (somatic, visceral, or autonomic), and the complexity of the neural circuit (monosynaptic or polysynaptic). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the difference between monosynaptic and polysynaptic spinal reflexes?","choices":["Monosynaptic involves a single segment of the spinal cord; polysynaptic integrates motor output from several spinal segments","Monosynaptic integrates several spinal segments; polysynaptic involves only a single spinal cord segment","Monosynaptic requires cortical processing; polysynaptic is processed entirely within the brainstem","Monosynaptic involves only cranial reflex circuits; polysynaptic involves only autonomic ganglia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Monosynaptic spinal reflexes involve a single segment of the spinal cord, while polysynaptic reflexes integrate motor output from several spinal segments. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the stretch reflex (e.g., the patellar reflex), and what is its pathway?","choices":["A stimulus compresses a tendon, activating a Golgi tendon organ; information is processed consciously in the cerebral cortex before the muscle relaxes","A stimulus compresses a muscle, directly activating a motor neuron with no sensory neuron or spinal processing involved","A stimulus stretches a muscle, activating a sensory neuron; information is processed in the spinal cord, motor neurons are activated, and the muscle (effector) contracts","A stimulus stretches a tendon, activating a Golgi tendon organ; this reflex occurs only in the upper limb, never the lower limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"In a stretch reflex, a stimulus stretches a muscle, activating a sensory neuron; information is processed in the spinal cord, motor neurons are activated, and the muscle (effector) contracts — the patellar reflex is a classic example, controlled by muscle spindles in the quadriceps femoris. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What role do higher centers play in reflexes, per the material?","choices":["Enhance or suppress spinal reflexes, and modify information carried in descending tracts of the spinal cord","Are the sole site of all reflex processing, entirely replacing the spinal cord's role in reflex circuits","Have no measurable influence on spinal reflexes or descending tract information","Only affect cranial nerve reflexes, never influencing spinal cord reflexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Higher centers can enhance or suppress spinal reflexes and modify information carried in the descending tracts of the spinal cord. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What structures make up a peripheral nerve, and how does the anatomy relate to the nerve plexuses previously described?","choices":["Bundled axons (organized similarly to bundled muscle fibers), which combine within plexuses to form the named peripheral nerves like the radial, femoral, and sciatic nerves","Only neuron cell bodies clustered together into ganglia, with no axons, myelin, or connective tissue covering present at all","A bundled tract of white matter found exclusively within the central nervous system's spinal cord and brain, never in the periphery","A single unmyelinated axon traveling alone from spinal cord to target, with no connective tissue covering or bundling with other axons"],"answer":0,"exp":"A peripheral nerve consists of bundled axons, and within nerve plexuses, fibers from multiple spinal nerve roots combine and reorganize to form the named peripheral nerves (such as the radial, femoral, and sciatic nerves) that emerge from each plexus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
